# Data-tier endpoint config — DEV / in-cluster (minikube) environment.
#
# SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for which Oracle / Elasticsearch / Memcached the
# consumers in this cluster talk to. Swap the file (data-tier.qa.env /
# data-tier.prod.env via DATA_TIER_ENV=...) to point a host or all of them at
# external machines without touching any consumer code or manifest.
#
# How it works: each *_HOST is either the in-cluster Service name (default) or
# an external DNS hostname (an IP works too). up.sh reads this file and:
#   - in-cluster mode (HOST == service name): leave the kustomize-applied
#     ClusterIP Service + StatefulSet/Deployment alone.
#   - external mode  (HOST != service name): swap the Service to
#     `type: ExternalName, externalName: <HOST>` and scale the now-unused
#     in-cluster workload to 0 replicas. Consumers continue to use the bare
#     name (oracle:1521, elasticsearch:9200, memcached:11211) — only what
#     that name resolves to in cluster DNS changes.
#
# Port: ExternalName services do NOT remap ports. The external host must
# listen on the same port the in-cluster Service used (the *_PORT values
# below are documentation; the actual port lives in each consumer's URL/JDBC
# string). If your external runs on a different port, NAT/port-forward it on
# the external side, or run a small in-cluster proxy.
#
# No seeding for externals — this cluster does NOT run any schema/index
# bootstrap against an external host. When ORACLE_HOST points off-cluster,
# the in-cluster oracle StatefulSet is scaled to 0 so the seeded image's
# restore-oradata.sh never runs; the old oracle-seed Job is gone too. You are
# responsible for arriving with a populated Oracle (+ ES indices, if redirected).
# Likewise for ELASTICSEARCH_HOST / MEMCACHED_HOST.
#
# Tip — providing an external Oracle: `docker run` the baked image
# (keycloak-demo-db:seeded, built from db/Dockerfile) on any host with
#   -p 1521:1521 -v <named-vol>:/opt/oracle/oradata
# and set ORACLE_HOST below to that host. The schema + Flyway seeds + login
# hash are already inside the image, so no separate seeding step is needed.
#
# Switching back to in-cluster: set the *_HOST back to the service name and
# re-run up.sh — it detects the leftover ExternalName Service, deletes it,
# and lets the kustomize apply re-create the in-cluster default.

ORACLE_HOST=oracle
ORACLE_PORT=1521
# JDBC SERVICE_NAME (Oracle PDB name) + schema user. P1's hibernate template
# substitutes ${ORACLE_HOST}/${ORACLE_PORT}/${ORACLE_PDB}/${ORACLE_USER}.
# In-cluster defaults match the baked image (FREEPDB1 / gp). For an external
# host (e.g. the prod 192.168.1.42 has SERVICE_NAME=ORCL12VM), override here.
ORACLE_PDB=FREEPDB1
ORACLE_USER=gp
# ORACLE_PASSWORD is a credential and lives OUTSIDE this file. Provide it via
# shell env on the up.sh invocation:
#   ORACLE_PASSWORD='real-prod-pw' ./k8s/up.sh
# up.sh will then create a Secret oracle-creds; if unset, no Secret is created
# and the P1 entrypoint falls back to its default (gp123 — the in-cluster
# baked-image password).

ELASTICSEARCH_HOST=elasticsearch
ELASTICSEARCH_PORT=9200

MEMCACHED_HOST=memcached
MEMCACHED_PORT=11211
